{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791633733,"nonce":"aIahe8dxlulcQ7vfXCR1lIZsJwc=","title":"cn resizable panels are jumpy, lose their panels as an element, share state and never report a resize","message":"Reported: \"Resizable panels are jumpy just like sliders\".\n\n\n1. Each fixed-size panel was a `Stateful` with `.deps([size])`, so every drag event rebuilt the panel and its content. The handle was a second `Stateful`, rebuilt on drag start and end to change its colour. Same shape as the slider jank.\n2. Used as an element (`.child(resizable_group()..)`), the group built from a config with `panels: Vec::new()` (\"panels already consumed\"): an empty group. Only `.build()` kept the panels; cn_demo calls `.build()`, so it never showed there.\n3. With no `.key(..)`, `from_config` named the state with `InstanceKey::new(\"resizable\")`, whose location is inside `from_config`, so every unkeyed group in the app shared one set of panel sizes. The builder's own call-site key was stored and unused.\n4. `on_resize` was stored and never called.\n\n\n- A fixed panel's width (or height) is bound to its size state (`.w(\u0026size)`), so a drag patches it and runs layout, with no rebuild. Content is laid over the panel as before.\n- The handle is a plain `Div`; its bar's colour is a computed over the drag index.\n- Panels sit in a `RefCell` and are taken by whichever of `.build()` or the element path builds first.\n- The key falls back to the builder's call-site `InstanceKey`.\n- A drag writes only sizes that changed and then calls `on_resize` with all sizes.\n\n\n`blinc_cn/tests/resizable_in_place.rs`: a drag resizes the panel with no subtree rebuild (each event checked), bounds clamp, the handle is highlighted only while dragged, two unkeyed groups made in different places keep their own sizes, `on_resize` reports the drag, and a vertical group resizes heights. Against the old code (through `.build()`), the first three fail: nothing follows the drag without a rebuild. Giving every group one key fails the shared-state test; dropping the `on_resize` call fails its test.","files":null}]}